  "body": {
    "id": "prop:ou-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "When \\(d=1\\), \\(D=[0,1]\\), the sites are equally spaced, the stencil radii are integer lattice multiples, and \\(|\\mathcal G_{N,j}|/N\\to\\eta_j>0\\), the affine-stencil construction satisfies \\(q_N(p)\\to(1,2^{2p-1},4^{2p-1})^{\\mathsf T}\\) and \\(H_N\\to\\operatorname{diag}(\\eta_0^{-1},\\eta_1^{-1},\\eta_2^{-1})\\). In particular, if the balanced construction has common limiting stencil fraction \\(\\eta\\), then \\(H_N\\to\\eta^{-1}I_3\\). The profile construction remains nonsingular at \\(A=0\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "d",
      "D",
      "sites",
      "N",
      "alphaX",
      "alphaW",
      "H",
      "qcurve",
      "p",
      "A"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:dimension",
      "ass:domain",
      "ass:spectral-family",
      "ass:observation-law",
      "def:local-moments",
      "thm:uniform-experiment"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "On the equally spaced one-dimensional lattice, take \\(L\\) and the three radii \\(2^jL\\) to be integers.  Integer lattice radii and translation-equivariant tie-breaking make every selected triplet a common lattice translate of its target triplet, so \\(F_{Nja}w^0=0\\) and the projection formula gives \\(w_{Nja}=w^0=(-2,1,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\).  Homogeneity of the power kernels yields exactly\n\\[\n q_N(p)=(1,2^{2p-1},4^{2p-1})^{\\mathsf T}\n\\]\napart from the vanishing integer end-cell discrepancy.\n\nFor two affine-annihilating one-dimensional stencils whose support intervals are disjoint, \\(|x-y|\\) is affine separately in \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) on their product.  Hence its double filtered sum is exactly zero.  The spacing choice in the affine-stencil algorithm makes distinct supports disjoint, so\n\\[\n R_{N,ab}=0\\quad(a\\ne b),\n \\qquad R_{N,aa}=4(2^jL)\\quad(a\\in\\mathcal G_{N,j}).\n\\]\nSubstitution in the definition of \\(H_N\\) gives\n\\[\n H_{N,jk}=0\\quad(j\\ne k),\n \\qquad H_{N,jj}=\\frac{N}{|\\mathcal G_{N,j}|}.\n\\]\nThus, if \\(|\\mathcal G_{N,j}|/N\\to\\eta_j>0\\), then\n\\[\n H_N\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{diag}(\\eta_0^{-1},\\eta_1^{-1},\\eta_2^{-1}).\n\\]\nThe balanced three-color construction has \\(\\eta_0=\\eta_1=\\eta_2=:\\eta>0\\), and hence \\(H_N\\to\\eta^{-1}I_3\\).  The value of \\(\\eta\\) records the fixed macrocell spacing and the retained information fraction.  Finally the determinant computation in the moment-separation lemma shows that \\(\\mathbf1\\) stays uniformly outside every secant and tangent nuisance plane, so profiling remains nonsingular at \\(A=0\\).  This is the actual Ornstein--Uhlenbeck lattice reduction of the frozen affine-stencil algorithm.",
    "justification": "This reduction is the required sanity check and fixes the covariance and mean normalizations in a fully tractable special case.",
    "gap": "Known two-scale cancellation assumes \\(p\\) known; the three-scale profiled reduction remains regular for \\(\\beta\\) when \\(p\\) is unidentified.",
    "consumer": "It supplies a reproducible benchmark for implementations and for checking all constants in the general-design theorem.",
